※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `@[listable]` says an array handed where one of its elements is wanted is
※ walked, and the answers make an array of the same shape.  A lambda says it the
※ way a function does: written before the thing it describes.
※
※ **It is part of the type**, and has to be.  A lambda is nearly always called
※ through a name, and what a name holds is a type -- so a promise the type did
※ not carry would be one the caller never heard.  That is the argument a
※ reference already makes for `mut` being part of its type: both sides of the
※ call reach the thing, so the caller cannot be left to guess.  The type is
※ therefore written `@[listable] fn(u8) → u8`, with the same word in the same
※ place.
※
※ **Only what a caller reads off the type may be said there.**  `inline`,
※ `impure` and the rest are about a body, and a body is not what a name holds;
※ writing one before a lambda is an attribute that does not apply (3204).
※
※ **A listable function stands where a plain one is wanted**, dropping the walk:
※ it promises everything the plain one does and adds to it, which is the rule
※ `&mut T` follows where `&T` is wanted.  One way only -- a plain one put where
※ a listable one is wanted would be a promise nobody made.  The two are the same
※ two addresses in the same two registers, so nothing is emitted for it.
※
※ Compare: **APL**, **BQN** and **UIUA**, where every primitive walks and there
※ is nothing to write; **Julia**, whose `f.(v)` puts the mark at the call rather
※ than on the function, so the caller decides and no type carries it;
※ **NumPy**, whose `vectorize` wraps a function in another one, which is what
※ this would be if the answer had not been to put it in the type.

@[listable]
fn twice(a: u8) → u8:
    a + a

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ a lambda that walks, called with an array and with a single value
    let double: @[listable] fn(u8) → u8 = @[listable] λ a: u8 → u8:
        a + a
    let v: u8⟦3⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8⟧
    let doubled: u8⟦3⟧ = double(v)
    let one: u8 = double(7u8)
    ※ two parameters, one walked and one not, which is what makes
    ※ `scaled(v, 2u8)` mean what it looks like
    let scaled: @[listable] fn(u8, u8) → u8 = @[listable] λ a: u8, b: u8 → u8:
        a × b
    let up: u8⟦3⟧ = scaled(v, 2u8)
    ※ a table, walked twice because the parameter takes a single value
    let rows: u8⟦2, 3⟧ = ⟦⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8⟧, ⟦4u8, 5u8, 6u8⟧⟧
    let grown: u8⟦2, 3⟧ = double(rows)
    ※ one that brings something in with it, which changes nothing about the walk
    let by: u8 = 10u8
    let shifted: @[listable] fn(u8) → u8 = @[listable] λ a: u8 [by] → u8:
        a + by
    let moved: u8⟦3⟧ = shifted(v)
    ※ handed to a name that does not ask for the walk, which drops it
    let plain: fn(u8) → u8 = double
    let alone: u8 = plain(9u8)
    ※ and a function defined the ordinary way, unchanged
    let named: u8⟦3⟧ = twice(v)
    if doubled⟦0⟧ ≠ 2u8 ∨ doubled⟦2⟧ ≠ 6u8:
        1u6
    elif one ≠ 14u8:
        2u6
    elif up⟦1⟧ ≠ 4u8 ∨ up⟦2⟧ ≠ 6u8:
        3u6
    elif grown⟦0, 2⟧ ≠ 6u8 ∨ grown⟦1, 1⟧ ≠ 10u8:
        4u6
    elif moved⟦0⟧ ≠ 11u8 ∨ moved⟦2⟧ ≠ 13u8:
        5u6
    elif alone ≠ 18u8:
        6u6
    elif named⟦1⟧ ≠ 4u8:
        7u6
    else:
        0u6
